---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import VideoDrawer from "@/components/physmeter/VideoDrawer.astro";
import TaskOverview from "@/components/physmeter/TaskOverview.astro";
import Leaderboard from "@/components/physmeter/Leaderboard.astro";
import { paper, pipeline } from "@/data/physmeter";
import "@/styles/physmeter.css";
---

<Layout title={paper.name} description={paper.description}>
  <article class="phys-page">
    <header class="phys-hero">
      <a class="phys-back" href="/research">← Research</a>
      <div class="phys-eyebrow"><span>VIDEO GENERATION · PHYSICAL EVALUATION</span><span class="phys-badge">Working draft</span></div>
      <h1>{paper.name}<span aria-hidden="true">.</span></h1>
      <p class="phys-subtitle">{paper.subtitle}</p>
      <p class="phys-intro">{paper.description}</p>
      <p class="phys-meta">Navers Lab · Einsia.AI <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> {paper.date}</p>
    </header>

    <nav class="phys-nav" aria-label="Paper sections">
      <a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#method">Method</a><a href="#tasks">Task overview</a><a href="#leaderboard">Leaderboard</a><a href="#videos">Video explorer</a><a href="#resources">Resources</a>
    </nav>

    <Leaderboard />

    <section id="overview" class="phys-section phys-split">
      <div><p class="phys-kicker">02 / THE QUESTION</p><h2>Looking real.<br />Behaving physically?</h2></div>
      <div class="phys-prose"><p>A convincing video can still contain impossible motion, inconsistent reflections, or incorrect changes in liquid level. PhysMeter asks which physical relationships survive when a video model continues a controlled scene.</p><p>The benchmark pairs a first-frame image and a continuation prompt with task-specific, measurable criteria. Physical scores are grounded in observable constraints. The V3 evaluation first uses a vision-language model to check temporal consistency, then applies task-specific physical evaluators to videos that pass the gate.</p><p class="phys-note">This page follows the updated PhysMeter manuscript, its reported results, and its nine-category task taxonomy.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section id="method" class="phys-section">
      <p class="phys-kicker">03 / THE METHOD</p><h2>From a controlled scene to a physical check.</h2>
      <p class="phys-section-intro">One evaluation pipeline, with measurements tailored to each phenomenon.</p>
      <ol class="phys-pipeline">{pipeline.map((step, i) => <li><span class="phys-step">0{i + 1}</span><h3>{step.title}</h3><p>{step.text}</p><span class="phys-step-label">{step.label}</span></li>)}</ol>
      <div class="phys-method-notes">
        <div><h3>Work in pixels and frames</h3><p>Ratios and other scale-invariant constraints make many physical relationships testable without absolute length or time calibration. Camera motion and perspective still require controlled conditions.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Separate visibility from correctness</h3><p>The method distinguishes measurement coverage from physical agreement. A clearly tracked but incorrect trajectory is measurable; an extraction failure alone does not prove a physical violation.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>


    <TaskOverview />
    <VideoDrawer />

    <section id="resources" class="phys-section phys-split phys-resources">
      <div><p class="phys-kicker">06 / PAPER & MATERIALS</p><h2>Follow the research.</h2></div>
      <div><a class="phys-resource-link" href={paper.pdf} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><div><strong>Read the working draft</strong><span>PDF · {paper.date}</span></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><p class="phys-note">The V3 task overview, paper-results leaderboard, and feature-extraction video explorer are available above. Code releases will be linked as they become available.</p></div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
